NNAAMMEE
     nnttppdd — Network Time Protocol (NTP) daemon

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     nnttppdd [--ddnnvv] [--ff _f_i_l_e]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The nnttppdd daemon synchronizes the local clock to one or more remote NTP
     servers or local timedelta sensors.  nnttppdd can also act as an NTP server
     itself, redistributing the local time.  It implements the Simple Network
     Time Protocol version 4, as described in RFC 5905, and the Network Time
     Protocol version 3, as described in RFC 1305.  Time can also be fetched
     from TLS HTTPS servers to reduce the impact of unauthenticated NTP man-
     in-the-middle attacks.

     The options are as follows:

     --dd          Do not daemonize.  If this option is specified, nnttppdd will run
                 in the foreground and log to _s_t_d_e_r_r.

     --ff _f_i_l_e     Use _f_i_l_e as the configuration file, instead of the default
                 _/_e_t_c_/_n_t_p_d_._c_o_n_f.

     --nn          Configtest mode.  Only check the configuration file for va‐
                 lidity.

     --vv          This option allows nnttppdd to send DEBUG priority messages to
                 syslog.

     nnttppdd uses the adjtime(2) system call to correct the local system time
     without causing time jumps.  Adjustments of 32ms and greater are logged
     using syslog(3).  The threshold value is chosen to avoid having local
     clock drift thrash the log files.  Should nnttppdd be started with the --dd or
     --vv option, all calls to adjtime(2) will be logged.

     At boot, nnttppdd will stay for a maximum of 15 seconds in the foreground and
     make efforts to verify and correct the time if constraints are configured
     and satisfied or if trusted servers or sensors return results, and if the
     clock is not being moved backwards.

     After the local clock is synchronized, nnttppdd adjusts the clock frequency
     using the adjfreq(2) system call to compensate for systematic drift.

     nnttppdd is started at boot time by default via _n_t_p_d___f_l_a_g_s in _/_e_t_c_/_r_c_._c_o_n_f.
     See rc(8) and rc.conf(8) for more information on the boot process and en‐
     abling daemons.

     When nnttppdd starts up, it reads settings from its configuration file, typi‐
     cally ntpd.conf(5), and its initial clock drift from _/_v_a_r_/_d_b_/_n_t_p_d_._d_r_i_f_t.
     Clock drift is periodically written to the drift file thereafter.

FFIILLEESS
     /etc/ntpd.conf         Default configuration file.
     /var/db/ntpd.drift     Drift file.
     /var/run/ntpd.sock     Socket file for communication with ntpctl(8).

